From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Brady <brady@aioscilloscope.com>
Date: Thu, 29 Oct 2026 09:35:27 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 2/2] rockchip: rk3399: nova: a test option, TPL's LPDDR4 init
 under the watchdog with breadcrumbs

Copyright (C) 2026 Olympian LED, Inc.

NOVA_RK3399_TPL_TRACE (off) makes a TPL that hangs in its LPDDR4 init
reset itself and say where it stopped, with no UART and no power cycle.

TPL starts the RK3399 watchdog in tpl_board_init (GLB_RST_CON 0x3, both
watchdog pclks ungated in the SGRF, TF-A's own early-arming sequence),
measures pclk_wdt against the arch timer and sets a period of 5-10 s,
again at sdram_init's entry once the clock driver has set the GPLL.
Every step of the init (rank detect, pctl_start, the mode-register
read, each training, the rate switch, dram_all_config) writes a
breadcrumb into PMU_SYS_REG0-3, and TPL's entry (the BootROM's
boot-source word, whether OS_REG0 held the download flag, the
watchdog's rate and TOP) goes into PMUGRF OS_REG3. All five keep their
value across a dw_wdt reset and the stock loader chain into NovaOS
(measured on the DHO924S). Every busy-poll of the LPDDR4 path gets a
200 ms deadline that records the poll, the channel and the register,
then waits for the watchdog (or, if its counter never moved, makes the
first global software reset). The explicit hangs of pctl_start do the
same. When the RAM probe returns, the period goes to its longest for the
stages after TPL; the watchdog cannot be stopped.

The words are in asm/arch-rockchip/nova_tpl_trace.h. Without the
option every hook is an empty inline and the init is unchanged.

Signed-off-by: Brady <brady@aioscilloscope.com>
